        "title": "Why Is Momentum Conserved?",
        "excerpt":"This article explores the conservation of momentum using Lagrangian mechanics.   Momentum in physics   Momentum is defined by \\(\\mathbf{p}=m\\mathbf{v}\\). It is a vector quantity, so its direction matters, and its rate of change is the force, \\(\\dot{\\mathbf{p}} = \\mathbf{F}\\).   The total momentum of an isolated system, one with no external forces acting on it, is conserved. This is not an accident: it follows from translational symmetry, the fact that the laws of physics are the same everywhere in space.   Why should a symmetry guarantee a conservation law? Lagrangian mechanics, developed by Joseph-Louis Lagrange in 1788, gives a clean explanation. In the Lagrangian formulation, the equations of motion are   \\[\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{q}_j}\\right) = \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial q_j},\\]  where \\(\\mathcal{L}=T-V\\) and the \\(q_j\\) are generalised coordinates.   Suppose \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) does not depend on some coordinate \\(q_j\\) (such a coordinate is called cyclic or ignorable), so that \\(\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial q_j} = 0\\). Then   \\[\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{q}_j}\\right) = 0,\\]  and the generalised momentum \\(p_j = \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{q}_j}\\) is a constant of the motion.   For a free particle in one dimension, \\(T=\\frac{1}{2}m\\dot{x}^2\\) and \\(V=0\\), so \\(\\mathcal{L}=\\frac{1}{2}m\\dot{x}^2\\). This does not depend on \\(x\\), and the conserved quantity is \\(\\partial\\mathcal{L}/\\partial\\dot x = m \\dot{x}\\): the familiar momentum.   Concluding remarks   This is a special case of Noether’s theorem, which states that every continuous symmetry of a system leads to a conserved quantity.   Here the Lagrangian does not depend on the position \\(x\\), only on the velocity \\(\\dot x\\), and that absence produces a conserved (canonical) momentum. If the Lagrangian does depend on \\(x\\), for example for a particle in a position-dependent potential \\(V(x)\\), the corresponding momentum is no longer conserved.   A second conserved quantity, related to symmetry under translations in time, is the energy function   \\[h=\\sum_i\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{q}_i}\\, \\dot{q}_i - \\mathcal{L},\\]  which is conserved whenever \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) does not depend explicitly on time. For the free particle above, \\(h = T\\); more generally, when \\(T\\) is quadratic in the velocities and \\(V\\) does not depend on them, \\(h = T + V\\) is the total energy. The energy function leads naturally to Hamiltonian mechanics, a reformulation of Lagrangian mechanics.   Exercises      What else is conserved for the free particle above? (Secondary school)   Why is the potential taken to be zero? What would change if it were a non-zero constant? (Secondary school)   Write down the Lagrangian for a particle moving in a plane under a central force, and show that angular momentum is conserved. (Undergraduate, years 1–2)   () The Lagrangian of a charged particle in an electromagnetic field is \\(\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{r}, \\dot{\\mathbf{r}}, t)=\\frac{1}{2} m\\lvert\\dot{\\mathbf{r}}\\rvert^2-e\\left[\\phi(\\mathbf{r}, t)-\\dot{\\mathbf{r}} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}(\\mathbf{r}, t)\\right]\\). Show that the canonical momentum is \\(\\mathbf{p}=m\\mathbf{v}+e\\mathbf{A}\\). (Undergraduate, years 2–3*)   Changes on 25 October 2026   What was wrong. The post said the energy function \\(h\\) equals the kinetic energy \\(T\\). That is only true for a free particle. When the kinetic energy is quadratic in the velocities and the potential does not depend on them, \\(h = T + V\\), the total energy.   What changed. This is corrected and the conditions for \\(h\\) to be conserved are given. The electromagnetic example is tidied.  ","categories": [],
        "tags": ["Classical Mechanics"],
        "url": "/MomentumConservation/",
        "teaser": null
