        "title": "Solving Systems of Congruences: The Chinese Remainder Theorem",
        "excerpt":"A number leaves a remainder of \\(2\\) when divided by \\(5\\), a remainder of \\(1\\) when divided by \\(3\\), and a remainder of \\(4\\) when divided by \\(7\\). What are all such numbers?   Two congruences   Suppose we want to solve   \\[\\begin{aligned} x &amp;\\equiv a_1 \\pmod{n_1}, \\\\ x &amp;\\equiv a_2 \\pmod{n_2}, \\end{aligned}\\]  where \\(n_1\\) and \\(n_2\\) are coprime. By Bézout’s identity there are integers \\(m_1, m_2\\) with   \\[m_1 n_1+m_2 n_2=1 .\\]  Then a solution is   \\[x=a_1 m_2 n_2+a_2 m_1 n_1 .\\]  To check this, note that \\(m_2 n_2 = 1 - m_1 n_1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n_1}\\), so \\(x \\equiv a_1 \\pmod{n_1}\\); similarly \\(x \\equiv a_2 \\pmod{n_2}\\).   Example   Solve   \\[\\begin{aligned} x &amp;\\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}, \\\\ x &amp;\\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}. \\end{aligned}\\]  Bézout’s identity for \\(5\\) and \\(3\\) can be taken as   \\[5 \\cdot 5 + (-8) \\cdot 3 = 1,\\]  so \\(m_1 = 5\\) and \\(m_2 = -8\\). Then   \\[x = 2 \\cdot (-8) \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5 = -48 + 25 = -23 .\\]  Any two solutions differ by a multiple of both \\(5\\) and \\(3\\), hence by a multiple of \\(\\operatorname{lcm}(5, 3) = 15\\). So the general solution is   \\[x \\equiv -23 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15}.\\]  More congruences   The same method handles more congruences one at a time. Returning to the opening question,   \\[\\begin{aligned} x &amp;\\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}, \\\\ x &amp;\\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}, \\\\ x &amp;\\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}, \\end{aligned}\\]  the first two congruences combine to \\(x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15}\\), so it remains to solve   \\[\\begin{aligned} x &amp;\\equiv 7 \\pmod{15}, \\\\ x &amp;\\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}. \\end{aligned}\\]  Now \\(1 \\cdot 15 + (-2) \\cdot 7 = 1\\), so \\(m_1 = 1\\), \\(m_2 = -2\\), and   \\[x = 7 \\cdot (-2) \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 15 = -98 + 60 = -38 .\\]  Hence the solution is   \\[x \\equiv -38 \\equiv 67 \\pmod{105}.\\]  Indeed, \\(67 = 13 \\cdot 5 + 2 = 22 \\cdot 3 + 1 = 9 \\cdot 7 + 4\\).   Conclusion   This is an instance of the Chinese remainder theorem: if \\(n_1, \\ldots, n_k\\) are pairwise coprime, the system \\(x \\equiv a_i \\pmod{n_i}\\) has a solution, unique modulo \\(n_1 n_2 \\cdots n_k\\). The theorem has applications throughout number theory, modular arithmetic and cryptography; for example, it is used to speed up RSA decryption.   Changes on 25 October 2026   What was wrong. Nothing. The explanation of why the formula works was brief.   What changed. I added that explanation and a check that 67 satisfies all three congruences. Previously titled “Solving a system of Congruences”.  ","categories": [],
        "tags": ["Number Theory"],
        "url": "/ChineseRemainder/",
        "teaser": null
