    "excerpt":"Publications      A Model-Agnostic Post-Processor for Tropical Cyclone Intensity and Central Pressure in Machine-Learning Weather Forecasts   Henry Yip et al.   Submitted, 2026      An Analysis of the Restricted Euler Problem Using Symplectic Integrators   Henry Yip and Jennifer M. Smillie   Edinburgh Student Journal of Science 1(3), 2025        doi:10.2218/esjs.10064     Journal page      Written up from my first research project, an eight-week summer project in 2024: an early, exploratory piece of work.      The Three-Body Problem is far from fully solved despite centuries of effort. The restricted Euler Problem is a special case in which two bodies are fixed in place, resulting in two Poisson-commuting conserved quantities, allowing the system to be fully integrable by the Liouville–Arnold theorem. We analysed the restricted Euler problem using an order-4 symplectic integrator, which conserves the Hamiltonian. We used this integrator to simulate the restricted Euler problem and recovered known orbits from the literature.    Hong Kong Observatory internship (June–September 2026)   I developed a model-agnostic machine-learning post-processing pipeline that improves tropical cyclone intensity forecasts from global AI weather models, correcting systematic biases in maximum wind speed and minimum sea-level pressure. A first-author manuscript has been submitted to a peer-reviewed journal, and I will link it here once the preprint is public.   Computational statistical physics (2026): the 3D Heisenberg model   My final project for Computational Statistical Physics at ETH Zürich, which received full marks: a Monte Carlo study of the three-dimensional classical Heisenberg model, measuring the critical temperature and five critical exponents with Metropolis and Wolff cluster simulations on lattices up to 256³ spins, all within 1σ of the 3D O(3) universality class.   Interactive write-up   Semester project (2026): the resolution dependence of environmental tropical cyclone metrics   A semester project in the Atmospheric Physics group at ETH Zürich, supervised by Prof. Ulrike Lohmann and Dr Bernhard Enz. Using the ERA5 ensemble reanalysis over the western tropical Atlantic, I looked at how the potential intensity of tropical cyclones depends on the spatial and temporal resolution of its inputs.   Background and interactive figures   Summer research project (2025): synchronisation in 2D turbulence   I studied the synchronisation of small scales in two-dimensional turbulence driven by Kolmogorov forcing, using JAX-CFD, and checked my code against results in the literature. I am now extending the theory to include Coriolis effects on the beta-plane. The work is supervised by Dr Jacob Page, with assistance from Dr Dmitriy Zhigunov.   JAX-CFD and Dedalus on Kolmogorov flow   Summer research project (2024): the three-body problem   An eight-week project in the School of Physics and Astronomy, University of Edinburgh, supervised by Prof. Jenni Smillie.   The project had two aims: to reproduce and visualise important orbits of the three-body problem, and to understand two landmark theoretical results, Chenciner and Montgomery’s proof that the figure-eight orbit exists and the convergence of Sundman’s series. I wrote symplectic integrators up to fourth order, used them to study the Lagrange, figure-eight and Broucke–Hadjidemetriou–Hénon orbits and Euler’s problem of two fixed centres, and ended with a search for new well-behaved orbits.   Interactive write-up Final report (PDF) Visualisation with Allison Lau   Research log   I kept a public log while the project ran. It starts from first simulations and works up to the existence proof of the figure-eight orbit:       Plotting and Visualising the Three-Body Problem 24 Jun 2024    Symplectic Integrators 25 Jun 2024    Ellipses and the Two-Body Problem 27 Jun 2024    Problem of Two Fixed Centres (Part I) 28 Jun 2024    Understanding and Visualising the Shape Sphere 4 Jul 2024    Problem of Two Fixed Centres (Part II) 9 Jul 2024    Sundman's Power Series Solution to the Three-Body Problem (Part I) 12 Jul 2024    Sundman's Power Series Solution to the Three-Body Problem (Part II) 16 Jul 2024    Proving the Figure-Eight Orbit Exists: Action Minimisation on the Shape Sphere 17 Jul 2024    Classifying and Finding New Possible Orbits (Part I) 22 Jul 2024    Problem of Two Fixed Centres (Part III) 26 Jul 2024    Background: Overview of the two-body problem.   Research problem: liquid slosh with pendulums and waves   This began as a group research project at the Edinburgh Scientific Researchers Association (ESRA), with Patrick Truong, Gregory Barbanel and Abdullah Fouzuddeen. Starting from a published single-pendulum model of liquid slosh in packaging machines, we looked at two ways of capturing the shape of the liquid surface rather than just its tilt: several coupled pendulums, and a wave equation for the surface.   The problem, the models and simulations  ","url": "https://henry-yip.github.io/research/"
